<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Wimpfen</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Bad Wimpfen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small imperial city on the Neckar, built beside the largest Hohenstaufen palace, that kept its freedom until 1803.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-wimpfen">Wimpfen</a> stands on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> near a Roman fort. Around 1200 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> built their largest imperial palace there, and the town that grew beside it received town rights. After the fall of the Hohenstaufen, Wimpfen counted as a free imperial city by about 1300, while Wimpfen im Tal below kept its collegiate church of Saint Peter.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> ran the city and its tiny territory, which lived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> shipping, crafts and farming. The town adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in the 16th century, though the collegiate foundation in the valley stayed Catholic. In 1622 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wimpfen">Battle of Wimpfen</a> was fought nearby, a victory for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-tserclaes-count-of-tilly">Tilly</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> army over Margrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-frederick-margrave-of-baden-durlach">George Frederick</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-baden-durlach">Baden-Durlach</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the mediatisation of 1802 and 1803 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-wimpfen">Wimpfen</a> passed briefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and then to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Hesse-Darmstadt</a>, remaining a Hessian exclave until after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Salt springs found in 1817 made it a spa, and as Bad Wimpfen it is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-wimpfen.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Wimpfen" width="320" height="173"><figcaption>Image: peter schmelzle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wimpfen-burgviertel-panoram.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bad Wimpfen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bad Wimpfen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1552&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-wimpfen" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Wimpfen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4891 461 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Wimpfen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5211 717L5229 694M5211 717L5198 698"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5198 698h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5211 717h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5211 717h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1552&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-wimpfen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
